cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this harness in research on AI-assisted software engineering,
  deterministic agent guardrails, or provenance-verified code generation,
  please cite it as below.
type: software
title: next-expo-supabase-agent-harness
abstract: >-
  A Next.js + Expo (React Native) monorepo starter on Supabase with a Claude
  Code harness: hooks that send the agent back to work while a quality gate
  fails. A command-line tool scaffolds a pnpm monorepo with a Next.js web app
  and an Expo app on one Supabase backend, where Postgres row-level security
  is the authorization boundary for both apps. Its premise is that quality is
  a deterministic gate, not a request: instruction files such as AGENTS.md and
  CLAUDE.md are advisory context, while hooks, gate scripts and CI enforce.
  Guard hooks (PreToolUse) check each shell command, file write and MCP call
  before it runs, and deny the ones a rule forbids. When the agent tries to
  end a turn, a Stop hook runs the validation chain, the row-level security
  tests, the unit suites and other checks, and sends the agent back while one
  fails, up to Claude Code's limit on consecutive blocks; the turn then ends
  and the next turn is told. Every gate in the chain has a can-fail proof, a
  registered test or CI step that shows it going red, and this repository's
  CI fails a change that adds a gate without one. The project's CI runs the
  same chain against a frozen list of its steps, so a project can add gates
  but cannot quietly remove one. pgTAP and supabase-js suites test the
  row-level security policies against a real Postgres database. An update
  command moves an existing project to a later release and can roll it back,
  and opt-in modules add web deployment, mobile builds and store submission,
  build provenance and building blocks for end-to-end encryption, among
  others. The harness is tamper-evident, not tamper-proof.
